The quintessential American dessert created at the Parker House Hotel in Boston. Despite its name, it is a tender double-layer sponge cake sandwiching rich egg-rich vanilla custard, draped with dark chocolate ganache.

*Adapted from [King Arthur Baking](https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/boston-cream-pie-recipe).*

## Ingredients

### Hot Milk Sponge Cake
- 2 cups (397g) granulated sugar
- 4 large eggs
- ⅓ cup (67g) vegetable oil
- 2 cups (240g) King Arthur Unbleached All-Purpose Flour
- 1 ¼ teaspoons table salt
- 2 teaspoons baking powder
- 4 tablespoons (57g) unsalted butter, at cool room temperature
- 1 cup (227g) whole milk
- 2 teaspoons pure vanilla extract

### Vanilla Pastry Cream Filling
- 2 ½ cups (567g) whole milk, divided
- ½ cup (99g) granulated sugar
- ¼ teaspoon table salt
- ⅓ cup (39g) cornstarch
- 3 large egg yolks
- 1 large whole egg
- 2 teaspoons pure vanilla extract

### Chocolate Ganache Glaze
- ¼ cup (57g) heavy whipping cream
- ⅓ cup (57g) dark chocolate chips or chopped semisweet chocolate
- ½ teaspoon pure vanilla extract

## Instructions

1. **Bake the Cake Layers:**
   - Preheat oven to 325°F (165°C). Grease two 9-inch round cake pans and line the bottoms with parchment paper rounds.
   - Beat the sugar and eggs on medium-high speed for 2 minutes until pale, thick, and ribbon-like. Beat in the vegetable oil.
   - Whisk together the flour, salt, and baking powder. Add to the egg mixture, beating just until incorporated.
   - In a small saucepan over medium heat, heat 1 cup milk and 4 tablespoons butter just until boiling. Remove from heat and stir in 2 teaspoons vanilla.
   - Gradually pour the hot milk mixture into the batter, mixing on low speed until smooth. The batter will be relatively thin.
   - Divide batter evenly between the prepared pans. Bake for 30 to 35 minutes until the tops feel springy and a tester comes out clean. Cool in pans for 10 minutes, then invert onto wire racks to cool completely.

2. **Make the Pastry Cream:**
   - In a saucepan, bring 2 cups of milk, ½ cup sugar, and ¼ teaspoon salt to a simmer, stirring to dissolve sugar.
   - In a medium bowl, whisk together the cornstarch, egg yolks, whole egg, and remaining ½ cup cold milk until smooth.
   - Slowly drizzle about half of the hot milk into the egg mixture while whisking vigorously to temper the yolks.
   - Pour the tempered mixture back into the simmering saucepan through a fine-mesh strainer.
   - Cook over medium heat, whisking constantly, until the mixture comes to a gentle boil and thickens noticeably (about 2 minutes). Remove from heat and whisk in 2 teaspoons vanilla.
   - Transfer to a shallow dish, press plastic wrap directly against the surface, and chill in the refrigerator until thoroughly cold and set.

3. **Assemble the Cake:**
   - Place the first cooled cake layer on a serving platter.
   - Whisk the chilled pastry cream briefly to smooth it, then spread in an even layer across the cake, keeping about ½ inch away from the edge.
   - Top with the second cake layer, pressing down gently.

4. **Make the Glaze and Finish:**
   - In a small heatproof bowl, microwave the heavy cream and chocolate in 20-second bursts, stirring until silky and smooth. Stir in ½ teaspoon vanilla.
   - Allow glaze to stand for 10 minutes at room temperature to thicken slightly.
   - Pour the glaze directly over the center of the cake, spreading it toward the perimeter with an offset spatula so it drips naturally over the sides.
   - Chill for 30 minutes to set the chocolate glaze before slicing.

## Notes

- **Make-Ahead Friendly:** The pastry cream can be made up to 2 days in advance and kept refrigerated.
- **Storage:** Store leftovers covered in the refrigerator for up to 4 days.
